{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"agent_message","text":"I’ve moved the task into review. I’m using the project-management skill because this verdict must be evidence-backed and persisted through the board lifecycle; architecture-diagram guidance isn’t relevant to this schema/test review. I’ll inspect the implementation and prior artifacts, run clean deterministic regeneration plus the relevant tests/lint, then record exactly one verdict without changing code."}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '1,240p' /Users/iv/.claude/skills/project-management/SKILL.md && sed -n '241,520p' /Users/iv/.claude/skills/project-management/SKILL.md\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '1,240p' /Users/iv/.claude/skills/project-management/SKILL.md && sed -n '241,520p' /Users/iv/.claude/skills/project-management/SKILL.md\"","aggregated_output":"---\nname: project-management\ndescription: >\n  File-based task boards and spawn-first multi-agent orchestration for AI agents.\n  Use when creating or maintaining boards, decomposing specs into Epic -> Story\n  -> Task/Bug work, tracking bugs, checking status/progress, or coordinating\n  execution through task-board. For board-driven execution, the active role of a\n  normal non-spawned session is ORCHESTRATOR (coordinator/supervisor): plan from\n  the critical path, create and refine board items, then delegate research,\n  implementation, testing, docs, and review to tracked background child runs\n  with explicit role, model, and reasoning selections; child-agent type follows\n  the current orchestrator runtime unless the human or task context explicitly\n  overrides it. Before launch,\n  inspect the project spawn policy: spawn.enabled can disable all child runs and\n  per-agent model_criterion=equal pins the executable model without model ranking;\n  ordered criteria use provider-local model ranks and require deliberate\n  one-line selection rationale plus content-bound acknowledgement after any\n  requested-to-resolved adjustment.\n  Spec-to-board decomposition is the Orchestrator's inline duty: never create a\n  meta-task or spawn solution-architect solely for board layout. Do not perform\n  task work inline unless the human explicitly asks for inline execution, except\n  for this mandatory board-layout duty.\n  Russian triggers: борд, доска, задачи,\n  эпик, стори, таски, скил, спавн, оркестратор, субагент, агенты, критический\n  путь, декомпозиция, статус, прогресс, гол, голы, цель.\ntriggers:\n  - creating task board from spec\n  - breaking down spec into tasks\n  - project planning\n  - task management\n  - creating bugs\n  - bug tracking\n  - epic, story, task, board, task-board\n  - борд, доска, задачи, эпик, стори, таски\n  - спека, требования, декомпозиция, разбей на задачи\n  - что дальше, какие задачи, статус, прогресс, покажи борд\n  - spawn, orchestrator, coordinator, sub-agent, multi-agent\n  - goal, delivery goal, goal-scope, goal-bound pool\n  - спавн, оркестратор, координатор, субагент, агенты, критический путь\n  - гол, голы, цель, взять пул под гол\n  - work planning, what's next, status, progress, summary\n---\n\n# Project Management Skill\n\nFile-backed project management via `task-board` plus tracked child-process orchestration. Board state lives in git or a board-server; executable work is delegated through role-based spawned agents.\n\n## Active Role: Orchestrator\n\nYour active role is **Orchestrator**. In the `task-board` role registry, `orchestrator` and `coordinator` are the same supervisor identity; `coordinator` is the older implementation name.\n\nUnless the prompt explicitly gives you a spawned specialist role, `TASK_BOARD_RUN_ID` is set, or the human explicitly asks for inline execution, do not act as a worker. Act as the parent Orchestrator.\n\nThe Orchestrator does board and orchestration work only:\n- create/refine epics, stories, tasks, bugs, AC, checklist DoD, dependencies, and resources\n- inspect `project_config()`, `summary()`, `plan(..., active=true)`, `agents()`, and `spawn` run state\n- choose the first unblocked critical-path work and spawn the right specialist roles\n- own each selected item through producer -> reviewer -> rework -> reviewer cycles until a reviewer accepts it and the board reaches `done`\n- summarize child outcomes for routing/status and report status without delegating routine review back to the human\n\n### Inline Spec Decomposition\n\nSpec decomposition into board elements is the Orchestrator's own inline duty. Read the spec and directly create or refine its epics, stories, tasks/bugs, acceptance criteria, checklist DoD, and dependencies with `task-board`. This board-layout work is an explicit exception to the normal specialist-delegation rule.\n\nDo not create a meta-task whose sole purpose is to lay out a spec on the board. Do not spawn `solution-architect` merely to translate a settled spec into stories and tasks. A `solution-architect` spawn is valid only when the work contains a genuine architecture decision; after that decision, the Orchestrator still owns the resulting board layout inline.\n\nDelegation examples:\n\n- Invalid: create a `decompose-auth-spec` meta-task and spawn `solution-architect` solely to turn the settled auth spec into stories and tasks.\n- Invalid: spawn `solution-architect` to copy already-settled requirements, acceptance criteria, and dependencies into board elements.\n- Valid: spawn `solution-architect` to choose between event-driven and request/response integration across modules, then use the decision while laying out the board inline.\n- Valid: spawn `solution-architect` to define an ambiguous cross-module contract and ownership boundary before refining the affected stories and tasks inline.\n\nThis mandate avoids orchestration work that produces no executable scope: the wordstats probe spent two full `sol/max` sessions and 25 minutes on meta-delegated decomposition before the first real task existed.\n\nThe Orchestrator does not do specialist task work inline. The board-layout duty above is the explicit exception. Decision rule: if the next step would produce or validate a project artifact — research, code/config changes, tests, documentation, synthesis/decision docs, or a review verdict — prepare a clear board item and run a tracked background specialist:\n\n```bash\n# With no provider system or legacy preference: Codex keeps Codex affinity\ntask-board spawn TASK-260218-abc123 --role developer --background --model gpt-5.6-sol --reasoning-effort max\n\n# With no provider system or legacy preference: Claude keeps Claude affinity\ntask-board spawn TASK-260218-def456 --role researcher --background --model claude-opus-4-8\n\n# Explicit human/task-context override: pass --agent and override affinity\ntask-board spawn TASK-260218-ghi789 --role reviewer --background --agent claude --model claude-opus-4-8\n```\n\nUse `task-board spawn wait RUN-...` only at real dependency barriers. Parallelize independent work in the same phase; sequence producer -> reviewer -> dependent consumer work.\n\n## Delivery Ownership: Close the Loop\n\nOnce execution starts, the Orchestrator remains responsible for delivery:\n\n1. Monitor the producer until it reaches `to-review` with outcome evidence.\n2. Spawn and monitor a reviewer child. `to-review` is an immediate routing trigger, never a stopping point or a request for human review.\n3. If changes are requested, preserve the verdict as rework context, route to `to-dev` or `analysis`, and spawn the appropriate producer.\n4. When rework returns to `to-review`, spawn a new reviewer. Repeat until the reviewer accepts and the board item is `done`.\n5. If any child exits before its role handoff or leaves the item parked in `analysis`, `development`, `testing`, or `reviewing`, inspect the run and outcomes, then retry or reroute through a focused child. Do not hand routine recovery to the human.\n6. Continue dependent work and close eligible parents. Report completion only after accepted `done` is real on the board.\n\nNever ask the human to perform routine code, test, documentation, research, or acceptance review. Human review is valid only when a task explicitly defines a human approval boundary. Requested changes, failed tests, recoverable child exits, and retryable runtime failures are orchestration work: retry, reroute, or spawn focused rework and keep going.\n\n## Project-State Monitoring\n\nUse the observability preset family for the Orchestrator's broad-to-narrow project-state loop. These are field presets on the existing query operations, not new operations:\n\n| Level | Query | Top-level projection |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Project | `summary() { observability-project }` | `observability` |\n| Epic | `list(type=epic) { observability-epic }` or `get(EPIC-...) { observability-epic }` | `id`, `name`, `status`, `observability` |\n| Story | `get(STORY-...) { observability-story }` | `id`, `name`, `status`, `observability` |\n| Task/Bug | `get(TASK-...) { observability-task }` | `id`, `name`, `status`, `observability` |\n\nUse compact output for routine monitoring:\n\n```bash\ntask-board q --format compact 'summary() { observability-project }'\ntask-board q --format compact 'list(type=epic) { observability-epic }'\ntask-board q --format compact 'get(STORY-260218-abc123) { observability-story }'\ntask-board q --format compact 'get(TASK-260218-def456) { observability-task }'\n```\n\n`observability-task` is also the leaf preset for Bugs. All four presets expose one stable nested shape:\n\n- `observability.scope` is `{type:\"project\", name:\"Project\"}` at project level and `{id, type, name}` for an element subtree.\n- `observability.summary` preserves the existing summary shape, scoped to the project or selected element plus its descendants: `byType` status counts, `active` element rows, and `blocked` rows with `blockedBy`, `derivedBlockedBy`, `isBlocked`, and `isAdvisoryBlocked`.\n- `observability.readiness.estimateKind` is the leaf or calculated estimate state: `notEstimated`, `estimated`, or `partiallyEstimated` as applicable.\n- `observability.readiness.coverage` always contains numeric `estimated`, `unestimated`, and `total`, string `ratio` such as `\"2/3\"`, and boolean `complete`.\n- `observability.readiness.rolledEstimate` is always the known sum shape `{kind:\"estimated\", scale:{kind:\"fibonacci\", number:N}}`, including when coverage is partial.\n- `observability.readiness.isBlocked` / `blockedElementIds` aggregate hard blocking in the scope. `isAdvisoryBlocked` / `advisoryBlockedElementIds` separately aggregate derived cross-container waiting. The ID arrays are sorted.\n\nPlain `summary()` keeps its legacy `{summary:{byType, active, blocked}}` response. Select `observability-project` when the readiness fields are needed.\n\n### Monitoring and routing loop\n\n1. **Project readiness:** start with `summary() { observability-project }`. Read `summary.active` for work that needs supervision or review routing, `summary.blocked` for blocker details, and `readiness` for aggregate estimate coverage and hard/advisory state. This is the fastest board-wide routing view.\n2. **Epic readiness:** list `observability-epic` rows to identify which delivery subtree owns a partial-coverage or blocker signal. Compare the top-level Epic `status` with its scoped `summary.active`; a quiet-looking container can still have active descendants, and a live container can still contain uncovered estimates.\n3. **Story readiness:** query the affected Story with `observability-story`. Story coverage is over its direct Task/Bug leaves; use the scoped active and blocked rows to decide which producer, reviewer, or blocker should move next.\n4. **Task/Bug readiness:** query the next leaf with `observability-task`. Coverage `1/1` plus no hard block means its estimate precondition is covered, but launch order still comes from dependencies and `plan(..., active=true)`. Coverage `0/1` or `estimateKind=notEstimated` means a new leaf needs an explicit estimate before entering `development`.\n\nWhen any project, Epic, or Story view reports `coverage.complete=false` or `estimateKind=partiallyEstimated`, do not call the rolled estimate a full-scope estimate. Narrow to incomplete Epics/Stories, then request the separate estimate projection:\n\n```bash\ntask-board q --format compact 'get(EPIC-260218-abc123) { estimate }'\ntask-board q --format compact 'get(STORY-260218-def456) { estimate }'\n```\n\nThe partial `estimate` projection adds the exact `notEstimatedCount` and sorted `unestimatedTaskIds` to its known `rolledEstimate`. Set or route estimates for those concrete Task/Bug IDs before using the roll-up for capacity or scope decisions. Missing estimates are missing coverage, not zero-point work. Legacy leaves without a persisted Estimate section remain start-compatible, but still appear as unestimated in container projections.\n\nRead blocker signals independently:\n\n- `isBlocked=true` means at least one scoped element is hard-blocked; inspect `blockedElementIds` and the matching `summary.blocked[*].blockedBy`, then advance those manual blockers before launching the dependent leaf.\n- `isAdvisoryBlocked=true` means at least one scoped element is waiting because of derived cross-container ordering; inspect `advisoryBlockedElementIds` and `derivedBlockedBy`, schedule the source first, but do not treat advisory state as status enforcement.\n- Both flags may be true in the same scope. Never collapse a mixed signal into one generic blocked label.\n\n`coverage.complete` describes estimate coverage only; it is not a delivery-ready or accepted verdict. `rolledEstimate` sums all known scoped leaf estimates regardless of lifecycle status, so it is not remaining work. Combine observability with `plan(..., active=true)` for waves/critical path, `agents()` for assignment state, and `spawn status/events/watch/wait` for child-run health.\n\n## Stop-The-Line: No Forced Fits\n\nDo not keep implementing when the task starts requiring a forced fit. A forced fit is any path where the agent has discovered that the requested behavior conflicts with a platform/API constraint, product decision, UX state model, ownership boundary, or architecture, and the remaining \"solution\" is mostly compensating hacks.\n\nThe Bluetooth permission/off-state failure pattern is the canonical warning sign: once the agent learned that iOS could not reveal powered-off Bluetooth state before permission without touching CoreBluetooth and potentially triggering the system prompt, it should have stopped, documented the constraint and options, and asked for a product decision. Adding more booleans, stubs, priority rules, and UI tests only made an invalid model look temporarily testable.\n\nPause or escalate only for either an evidence-backed external blocker that cannot be resolved autonomously or an evidence-backed unresolved human-only boundary—an impossible platform/API or product constraint, approval boundary, or unresolved product or architecture tradeoff—that cannot be derived from the task, AC, or existing decisions.\n\nWhen this happens:\n- Stop changing product code before adding another workaround layer.\n- Record the constraint, evidence, failed assumptions/attempts, viable alternatives and tradeoffs, recommended option, and exact decision or external input needed on the board as notes or an outcome resource.\n- Mark the task `blocked` and request only that exact decision or external input. Do not use `blocked` for recoverable failures or ordinary rework.\n- Resume only after the product/architecture decision is explicit, or after you find a genuinely clean design that does not depend on the broken assumption.\n\nTests and stubs are not proof that a forced-fit design is correct. They are useful only after the state model and platform assumptions are valid.\nRoutine review, rework, test failures, recoverable child-process failures, and implementation choices resolvable from existing requirements are not reasons to hand control back to the human.\n\n---\n\n# Part 1: Task Management\n\n---\n\n## When to Use\n\n- New project with SPEC/requirements → break down into epics/stories/tasks\n- Need progress tracking in files\n- Working with AI agent on a project\n- Tracking bugs\n\n---\n\n## CLI Tool: `task-board`\n\nAll board operations go through the `task-board` CLI. No manual file editing.\n\n**Source repo:** your local `skill-project-management` checkout\n**Installed skill artifact:** `~/.agents/skills/project-management` (degitized copy for agent runtimes)\n**In PATH:** installed copy in `~/.local/bin/task-board`\n\nInstall/update the local binary before board setup:\n\n```bash\ncd /path/to/skill-project-management\n./scripts/setup.sh\n```\n\nFor a safe reinstall that refreshes binaries, the installed skill copy, roles, and install metadata without touching the current project's `task-board.config.json`, use:\n\n```bash\n./scripts/setup.sh --install-only\n```\n\n`--install-only` is also the supported verification gate for rollout drift. It checks the installed `task-board` binary with `project_config()` and local `models()` queries, requires the tracked background, explicit-selection, and child launch-composition contract fields plus current Codex registry to be present, verifies the installed `SKILL.md` matches this source artifact, and fails loud if PATH resolves a stale binary.\n\n`task-board self-update` relies on `~/.config/task-board/install.json`, which is written by `scripts/setup.sh`. Do not treat `~/.agents/skills/project-management` as the update source; it is the installed artifact copy and has git metadata scrubbed.\n\n`scripts/setup.sh` is the single supported install/update entrypoint for `task-board`, `task-board-tui`, and the installed skill artifact. `make install-*` targets are binary build helpers and do not replace setup.\n\nWhen you bootstrap board-driven work inside a real code repository, prefer a single source-checkout setup command that also bootstraps the repo-local agent runtime through `relux-agents-infra`:\n\n```bash\ncd /path/to/skill-project-management\n./scripts/setup.sh --project-dir /path/to/project --mode local --board-dir .task-board --bootstrap-local-agents\n```\n\n`--bootstrap-local-agents` runs `agents-infra setup local /path/to/project` after writing the project config. Install the global `agents-infra` launcher once from the `relux-agents-infra` source repo via `./setup.sh`, then use this shortcut for project repos.\n\nThat local setup makes project `.agents/` the installed runtime copy and refreshes `.claude/` + `.codex/` as thin shims into it. Do this right after board/config setup so spawned agents see the repo-local instructions, skills, and templates instead of drifting onto only global state.\n\n### Quick Reference\n\nAll reads use `q` (query DSL), all writes use `m` (mutate DSL). Compact format (`--format compact`) saves tokens.\n\n```bash\n# Queries (reads)\ntask-board q 'schema()'                                                          # discover fields, presets, operations, mutations\ntask-board q 'project_config()'                                                  # effective runtime + spawn.enabled and per-agent model criteria; run before spawning/audits\ntask-board q --format compact 'summary()'                                        # board overview (counts + active/blocked)\ntask-board q --format compact 'get(TASK-260218-abc123) { full }'                 # full element details\ntask-board q --format compact 'list(type=task, status=development) { overview }'  # filtered list\ntask-board q --format compact 'list(type=task, blocked=true) { id name status }'  # blocked tasks\ntask-board q --format compact 'list(type=story, parent=EPIC-260218-abc123) { overview }'  # stories in epic\ntask-board q --format compact 'agents()'                                         # agent dashboard\ntask-board q --format compact 'models(agent=codex)'                             # local spawn model/effort registry (also in remote board mode)\ntask-board q --format compact 'models(agent=qwen)'                              # Qwen model/effort/context/Token Plan registry\ntask-board q --format compact 'plan(EPIC-260218-abc123, mode=children)'          # execution waves (super-compact planning shape)\ntask-board q 'plan(scope=[EPIC-260218-abc123, STORY-260218-def456], mode=children)'  # plan over a set of subtrees (union)\ntask-board q --format compact 'count(type=task, status=done)'                    # count elements\n\n# Mutations (writes)\ntask-board m 'create(type=epic, name=my-feature, description=\"...\")'                        # create epic\ntask-board m 'create(type=story, parent=EPIC-260218-abc123, name=audio-capture)'            # create story\ntask-board m 'create(type=task, parent=STORY-260218-abc123, name=impl, description=\"...\")'  # create task\ntask-board m 'set_details(TASK-260218-abc123, title=\"new title\", description=\"...\")'        # update README fields\ntask-board m 'rename(TASK-260218-abc123, name=better-task-name)'                            # update overview name/slug without changing ID\ntask-board m 'set_estimate(TASK-260218-abc123, estimate=estimated, scale=fibonacci, number=5)'  # estimate Task/Bug work\ntask-board m 'set_status(TASK-260218-abc123, status=development)'                           # change status\ntask-board m 'set_status(STORY-260218-abc123, status=done, commit_ack=scope_committed)'      # acknowledge an enforced version-control done transition\ntask-board m 'assign(TASK-260218-abc123, agent=claude-opus)'                                # assign agent\ntask-board m 'unassign(TASK-260218-abc123)'                                                 # remove assignment\ntask-board m 'link(TASK-260218-def456, blocked_by=TASK-260218-abc123)'                      # add dependency\ntask-board m 'unlink(TASK-260218-def456, blocked_by=TASK-260218-abc123)'                    # remove dependency\ntask-board m 'check_item(TASK-260218-abc123, item=1)'                                       # check checklist item\ntask-board m 'add_checklist_item(TASK-260218-abc123, text=\"Write tests\")'                   # add checklist item\ntask-board m 'set_notes(TASK-260218-abc123, text=\"Progress update\")'                        # append notes\ntask-board m 'set_notes(TASK-260218-abc123, text=\"Full replacement\", set=true)'             # replace notes\ntask-board m 'set_parent(TASK-260218-abc123, parent=STORY-260218-def456)'                   # move to new parent\ntask-board m 'add_resource(TASK-260218-abc123, name=TASK-260218-abc123_design.md, content=\"...\", type=precondition, description=\"Architecture design\")'  # create resource\ntask-board m 'update_resource(TASK-260218-abc123, name=TASK-260218-abc123_design.md, content=\"revised\", type=precondition, description=\"Architecture design v2\")'  # replace resource\ntask-board m --dry-run 'delete(TASK-260218-abc123)'                                         # preview destructive op\ntask-board m --confirm 'delete(TASK-260218-abc123)'                                         # execute destructive op\n\n# Batching (semicolons)\ntask-board m 'assign(TASK-260218-abc123, agent=claude-opus); set_status(TASK-260218-abc123, status=development)'\n\n# Spawn tracked background runs (--role, --background, and --model are REQUIRED; Codex/Qwen also require --reasoning-effort)\ntask-board spawn TASK-260218-abc123 --role developer --background --agent codex --model gpt-5.6-sol --reasoning-effort max\ntask-board spawn TASK-260218-abc123 --role researcher --background --agent codex --model gpt-5.6-sol --reasoning-effort max\ntask-board spawn TASK-260218-abc123 --role reviewer --background --agent codex --model gpt-5.6-sol --reasoning-effort max\ntask-board spawn TASK-260218-abc123 --role developer --background --agent codex --model gpt-5.6-sol --reasoning-effort max --instruction impl-guide.md\ntask-board spawn TASK-260218-abc123 --role developer --background --agent claude --model claude-opus-4-8 --budget 5.0\ntask-board spawn TASK-260218-abc123 --role developer --background --agent qwen --model qwen3.7-plus --reasoning-effort xhigh\ntask-board spawn TASK-260218-abc123 --role developer --background --agent codex --model gpt-5.6-terra --reasoning-effort max\ntask-board spawn TASK-260218-abc123 --role developer --background --agent codex --model gpt-5.6-luna --reasoning-effort max\ntask-board spawn TASK-260218-abc123 --role developer --background --agent codex --model gpt-5.3-codex-spark --reasoning-effort xhigh\ntask-board spawn status RUN-260320-abc123                                            # inspect tracked run state\ntask-board spawn wait RUN-260320-abc123                                              # block on tracked run state after RUN exists\ntask-board spawn events RUN-260320-abc123                                            # replay durable run events (supports --cursor/--follow)\ntask-board spawn watch RUN-260320-abc123                                             # follow durable run-event notifications\n\n# Auth (always local, never sent to server)\ntask-board login sampleuser tb_4lc6...                                        # store token in Keychain and select active user outside repo config\ntask-board login admin tb_UJTW... --remote https://board.example.com          # explicit remote bootstrap\ntask-board logout                                                              # remove selected user's token\ntask-board whoami                                                              # show remote URL + active user + token status\ntask-board switch-user admin                                                   # switch active user without touching versioned config\ntask-board q 'whoami()'                                                        # whoami via query DSL\ntask-board q 'project_config()'                                                # mode, board source, and effective spawn policy\ntask-board m 'login(user=sampleuser, token=tb_xxx)'                           # login via mutation DSL\ntask-board m 'switch_user(user=admin)'                                         # switch via mutation DSL\n\n# CLI utilities\ntask-board grep \"AudioRecorder\"                            # full-text search across board files (local only)\ntask-board validate                                        # check board structure (local only)\ntask-board repair-links                                    # list unsupported stored container links (local, read-only)\ntask-board repair-links STORY-260218-abc123 --confirm      # remove selected suspicious links\ntask-board tui                                             # interactive terminal dashboard\n```\n\n---\n\n## Task Board Structure\n\n### Hierarchy\n\n```\nEpic → Story → Task/Bug\n```\n\n- **Epic** = large feature / module\n- **Story** = specific functionality within epic\n- **Task** = atomic unit of work\n- **Bug** = defect tracked within a story\n\n### Naming Convention\n\n**Distributed IDs** — each element gets a unique ID based on date + random hash.\n\n```\nEPIC-260203-a1b2c3_recording/\n  STORY-260203-d4e5f6_audio-capture/\n    TASK-260203-g7h8i9_audiorecorder-interface/\n    TASK-260203-j0k1l2_implementation/\n    BUG-260203-m3n4o5_some-bug/\n```\n\nFormat: `{TYPE}-{YYMMDD}-{hash}_{kebab-case-name}`\n- `EPIC-YYMMDD-xxxxxx_name`\n- `STORY-YYMMDD-xxxxxx_name`\n- `TASK-YYMMDD-xxxxxx_name`\n- `BUG-YYMMDD-xxxxxx_name`\n\nWhere:\n- `YYMMDD` — creation date (year, month, day)\n- `xxxxxx` — 6-character base36 hash (collision-resistant)\n\nIDs are globally unique and work in parallel environments (no counter conflicts).\n\n### Directory Layout\n\n```\n.task-board/\n├── .resources/                   # Flat resource storage (managed by CLI)\n│   ├── TASK-260203-g7h8i9/       # Resources keyed by element ID\n│   │   ├── design.md             # Precondition: input for the task\n│   │   └── results.md            # Outcome: work product\n│   └── STORY-260203-d4e5f6/\n│       └── research.md\n├── EPIC-260203-a1b2c3_recording/\n│   ├── README.md                 # Epic description, scope, AC\n│   ├── progress.md               # Status, blockedBy, checklist, notes, resource refs\n│   ├── STORY-260203-d4e5f6_audio-capture/\n│   │   ├── README.md\n│   │   ├── progress.md\n│   │   ├── TASK-260203-g7h8i9_interface/\n│   │   │   ├── README.md\n│   │   │   └── progress.md\n│   │   ├── TASK-260203-j0k1l2_implementation/\n│   │   │   ├── README.md\n│   │   │   └── progress.md\n│   │   └── BUG-260203-m3n4o5_crash-on-start/\n│   │       ├── README.md\n│   │       └── progress.md\n│   └── STORY-260203-p6q7r8_amplitude/\n│       └── ...\n└── EPIC-260203-s9t0u1_storage/\n    └── ...\n```\n\nNo `system.md` needed — IDs are self-contained and don't require global counters.\n\n---\n\n## Statuses\n\n9 statuses: `backlog` → `analysis` → `to-dev` → `development` → `to-review` → `reviewing` → `done`. Plus `closed` and `blocked`.\n\nBefore a newly created Task/Bug enters `development`, set its estimate with\n`set_estimate(ID, estimate=estimated, scale=fibonacci, number=N)`. The start\ntransition returns a typed estimate-required error while the explicit value is\n`notEstimated`. Legacy Task/Bug files that have no Estimate section are\ngrandfathered and remain startable.\n\n`done` is accepted leaf work or computed container completion; `closed` is an\nexplicit administrative disposition. A story or epic may remain `closed` only\nwhile every direct and transitive descendant is `done` or `closed`.\n\nFor full status flow, auto-promotion/reopen, blocked vs is_blocked — see [`references/statuses.md`](references/statuses.md).\n\n---\n\n## File Formats\n\nEach element directory contains two files:\n- **README.md** — static description (what the element is, scope, acceptance criteria). Does not change during execution.\n- **progress.md** — dynamic lifecycle journal (status, Task/Bug estimate, assignment, timestamps, dependencies, checklist, notes). Updated throughout execution.\n\nBoth files use source-aware, injection-safe Markdown persistence. Unchanged\nbytes and custom sections are retained during rewrites; free-text payload fields\nround-trip byte-exact. Mutation boundaries normalize identifier-like fields\n(`AssignedTo`, `BlockedBy`, `Blocks`) by trimming boundary whitespace and reject\nempty or reserved `(none)` values with a typed error. Checklist text is rejected\nonly when empty-after-trim and is otherwise persisted verbatim.\n\nTask/bug dependencies are bidirectional ground truth. Story/epic ordering is derived from the live task DAG at read time; task links never materialize container side-effect links. Explicit stored container links remain manual state, while projections expose task-derived blockers separately.\n\nFor detailed format specifications, field descriptions, and examples, see [`references/file-formats.md`](references/file-formats.md).\n\n---\n\n## Resources & Bugs\n\nResources are linked to board elements via DSL mutations. Two types:\n- **Precondition** — inputs for the task (instructions, specs, design docs). Used by `task-board spawn` to compose agent prompts.\n- **Outcome** — work products (research results, implementation notes). Persisted for other agents/tasks to consume.\n\n```bash\n# Create resource\ntask-board m 'add_resource(TASK-260218-abc123, name=TASK-260218-abc123_design.md, content=\"...\", type=precondition, description=\"Architecture design\")'\ntask-board m 'add_resource(TASK-260218-abc123, name=TASK-260218-abc123_results.md, content=\"...\", type=outcome, description=\"Research findings\")'\n\n# Replace existing resource\ntask-board m 'update_resource(TASK-260218-abc123, name=TASK-260218-abc123_results.md, content=\"revised findings\", type=outcome, description=\"Research findings v2\")'\n\n# Delete resource\ntask-board m --confirm 'delete_resource(TASK-260218-abc123, name=TASK-260218-abc123_design.md)'\n```\n\nResources are stored flat in `.task-board/.resources/<elementID>/` and referenced from `progress.md`. Bugs live inside stories alongside tasks.\n\nFor resource workflow and bug tracking — see [`references/resources-and-bugs.md`](references/resources-and-bugs.md).\n\n---\n\n## Workflow\n\n### Full Development Cycle (MANDATORY)\n\nWhen the user asks to build/implement something, the agent MUST follow this end-to-end flow:\n\n#### Spec\n- Specs live in the `.spec/` directory (one or more `.md` files), not in a single `SPEC.md`\n- Create `.spec/` if it doesn't exist; add focused spec files per topic/feature (e.g. `.spec/auth.md`, `.spec/api.md`)\n- Clarify requirements with the user if anything is ambiguous\n- `.spec/` is the source of truth — all work traces back to it\n\n#### Plan on the Board\n- Create epics and stories: `task-board m 'create(type=epic, name=..., description=\"...\")'`\n- Refine each element: query with `task-board q 'get(ID) { full }'`, clarify gaps, update via `task-board m 'set_details(ID, description=\"...\")'`\n- Perform spec-to-board decomposition inline as the Orchestrator; do not create a meta-task or spawn `solution-architect` merely to lay out a settled spec\n- No element should have placeholder text — description and AC must be complete and unambiguous\n\n#### Story Detailing, Phase Planning, Execution (MANDATORY)\n\nAfter the Orchestrator completes story detailing inline, use [`references/planning-workflow.md`](references/planning-workflow.md) for phase planning, user approval, pre-launch checks, specialist execution, supervision, and visualization. Its legacy decomposition-spawn examples do not override this inline mandate and must not be used for settled-spec board layout.\n\nKey points:\n- **Story detailing before planning** — the Orchestrator creates and refines the required stories and tasks inline before phase planning\n- **No meta-delegated board layout** — do not create a meta-task or spawn `solution-architect` solely to translate a settled spec into board elements; reserve that role for genuine architecture decisions such as system-design tradeoffs or unresolved cross-module contracts\n- **Use `task-board q 'plan(...)'` as the only planning source** — default JSON returns nested phases and `criticalPath`; compact output returns flat `waves` and a single-line `criticalPath`. Unresolvable dependencies fail with a typed validation error instead of producing misleading phases. Agents never need a rendered graph to plan. Use `task-board plan --save` only to persist a human-readable snapshot; never build phases manually or write plan files by hand\n- **Dump plan to `.planning/`** — after planning, save to `.planning/{YYMMDD}_{HHmmss}_{topic}.md` for session recovery. Use `task-board plan --save`\n- **User approval MANDATORY** — never start execution without user confirmation\n- **Pre-launch checklist** — model, status, assignment, concrete IDs, first command\n- **Coordinator is SUPERVISOR** — never writes code, only delegates to sub-agents\n\n#### Research / Analysis Tasks\n\nResearch workflow, resource linking, document structure, status flow — see [`references/research-workflow.md`](references/research-workflow.md).\n\nKey points:\n- Research results are linked to board elements via `task-board m 'add_resource(..., type=outcome)'` on first write and `update_resource(...)` on revisions\n- Broadly useful research also persists in **`.research/`** directory (project-level)\n- Status flow: `backlog → analysis → to-review → reviewing → done` (skip `development`; after `to-review`, spawn an explicit reviewer such as `task-board spawn TASK-... --role reviewer --background --agent codex --model gpt-5.6-sol --reasoning-effort max`)\n- Always link research output as an outcome resource on the task, and include the task ID in the resource name\n\n### Iterative Refinement (for individual elements)\n\nWhen creating ANY element (epic/story/task/bug), the agent MUST:\n\n1. **Create** element via `task-board m 'create(type=..., name=..., description=\"...\")'`\n2. **Read** the element: `task-board q 'get(ID) { full }'`\n3. **Clarify** — if description is vague or has gaps, ask the user\n4. **Update** via `task-board m 'set_details(ID, description=\"...\", ac=\"...\")'`\n5. **Repeat** steps 2-4 until description is exhaustive\n6. Element is **ready** only when description and AC are complete and unambiguous\n\n### Project Start\n\n- Create elements: `task-board m 'create(type=epic, name=auth-system, description=\"...\")'`\n- Create stories: `task-board m 'create(type=story, parent=EPIC-260218-abc, name=login)'`\n- Detail tasks: `task-board m 'create(type=task, parent=STORY-260218-abc, name=impl)'`\n- Set dependencies: `task-board m 'link(STORY-260218-def, blocked_by=STORY-260218-abc)'`\n- **Generate phases**: `task-board q 'plan(EPIC-260218-abc, mode=children)'` — only after all stories have tasks and dependencies are set\n\n### Spawning Agents for Tasks\n\nThe **coordinator never writes code** — it delegates through tracked spawn commands with explicit role/model selection and runtime-affine agent selection.\n\nTreat [`references/tracked-background-spawn.md`](references/tracked-background-spawn.md) as the canonical operator-facing spawn contract. The guidance below is the workflow summary agents should follow.\n\n**`--role`, `--background`, and `--model` are REQUIRED.** Codex and Qwen spawns also require `--reasoning-effort`; Claude spawns do not accept it. Choose the role based on the task:\n\n`--agent` is optional by design. Canonical `spawn.preferred_agentic_system` is an enforced tagged union: `exclusive` pins one provider; `mixed` permits a normalized provider set and retains runtime affinity when it belongs to that set. An explicit `--agent` wins only within the configured set; an out-of-set explicit provider or affinity is refused before task lookup or spawn side effects and names the allowed providers. When the canonical system is absent, legacy `spawn.preferred_agent` retains its ceiling/executable-gated preference and affinity fallback; when both are absent, a Codex orchestrator session spawns a `codex` child and a Claude orchestrator session spawns a `claude` child. The implementation detects Codex from `CODEX_SESSION`, `CODEX_THREAD_ID`, or `CODEX_CI` and otherwise uses Claude.\n\nOptional `spawn.schedule.windows[]` overlays that base provider system during disjoint UTC windows. Each window is start-inclusive/end-exclusive, uses `HH:MM`, may cross midnight, and carries its own `preferred_agentic_system`; equal or overlapping bounds are invalid. The active window replaces the base system before provider and registered-model validation, while gaps use the base system or unrestricted runtime affinity when no base exists. Inspect `project_config().spawn.schedule` for the configured windows, `active_window`, and `base_preferred_agentic_system` before selecting a provider.\n\n### Goal-Capable Primary Sessions\n\nThe fixed primary-session UX is `agents-infra codex` for Codex and\n`agents-infra claude` for Claude. Both are fully capable of starting\ngoal-bound tracked owners through `task-board spawn --goal-scope`.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
