{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_7","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '1401,1532p' /Users/iv/.claude/skills/project-management/SKILL.md\"","aggregated_output":"8. Agent gets FIRST command (set `start_status` after launch) and LAST role-handoff command (set end status) in prompt\n\n```bash\n# Spawn researcher for a research task (status → analysis)\ntask-board spawn TASK-260218-abc --role researcher --background --agent codex --model gpt-5.6-sol --reasoning-effort max\n\n# Spawn developer for implementation (status → development, DoD → checklist)\ntask-board spawn TASK-260218-def --role developer --background --agent codex --model gpt-5.6-sol --reasoning-effort max\n\n# Agent type follows the active schedule window, canonical provider system, legacy preference, then runtime affinity; model/reasoning remain explicit\ntask-board spawn TASK-260218-xyz --role developer --background --model gpt-5.6-terra --reasoning-effort max\n\n# Spawn reviewer (status → reviewing)\ntask-board spawn TASK-260218-ghi --role reviewer --background --agent codex --model gpt-5.6-sol --reasoning-effort max\n\n# Spawn orchestrator/coordinator for decomposition / orchestration / verification\ntask-board spawn TASK-260218-jkl --role orchestrator --background --agent codex --model gpt-5.6-sol --reasoning-effort max\ntask-board spawn TASK-260218-jkl --role coordinator --background --agent codex --model gpt-5.6-sol --reasoning-effort max\n```\n\n`orchestrator` / `coordinator` is the special supervisor role: it decomposes settled specs into board elements inline, creates or refines tasks, spawns specialists, guards architecture, and owns producer -> reviewer -> rework -> reviewer cycles until accepted `done`. It must not create a board-layout meta-task, stop at `to-review`, ask the human to perform routine review, or do specialist implementation directly when delegation is appropriate. Spawn `solution-architect` only for genuine architecture decisions, not to perform the board layout.\n\nFor coordinator orchestration:\n- fan out children with explicit role/model selections; inspect `project_config().spawn.preferred_agentic_system`, omit `--agent` for exclusive/in-set-affinity resolution, and pass it only for an allowed human/task-context provider\n- keep the returned `RUN-...` ids\n- use `task-board spawn status/wait` as explicit synchronization points\n- sequence dependent work: wait for producer runs, verify outcome resources, then spawn reviewer/consumer runs\n- on requested changes, preserve the verdict, spawn focused rework, and send the next handoff through another reviewer until accepted `done`\n- if a child exits before handoff or parks in an intermediate status, inspect its run/outcomes and retry or reroute through a focused child; do not hand recoverable work to the human\n- use `blocked` only for a concrete external blocker or unresolved human-only platform/product/architecture/tradeoff/approval decision, after recording evidence, failed assumptions/attempts, viable alternatives and tradeoffs, the recommendation, and the exact decision or external input needed\n- do not shell-background `task-board spawn`; that duplicates the async layer and makes completion handling worse\n\n### Skill Injection\n\nSkills are injected as references (paths), not inlined into prompts. Claude, Codex, and Qwen use lazy body loading — only skill metadata (name + description) loads at startup (~50-200 tokens per skill). Treat spawned-role skill lists as lazy catalogs: full SKILL.md bodies load only when relevant to the concrete task scope, unless the user, task, or project explicitly requires one.\n\nThis means one `developer` role can reference many skills (swiftui, core-data, go-testing-tools, etc.) without context bloat — the agent picks what's needed for the specific task. See [`references/context-efficiency.md`](references/context-efficiency.md) for the selection rule.\n\n## Sub-Agent Prompt Templates\n\nFor sub-agent prompt templates (implementation agent and coordinator review workflow), see [`references/sub-agent-templates.md`](references/sub-agent-templates.md). Its legacy decomposition-agent template must not be used for spec-to-board layout. A `solution-architect` child produces only a genuine architecture decision; the Orchestrator applies that decision to the board inline.\n\nKey points covered there:\n- Reality check: how to maximize sub-agent compliance with task-board updates\n- Do not use the legacy decomposition-agent template for board layout; delegate only the architecture-decision deliverable that informs later inline board refinement\n- Implementation agent template (for execution phase)\n- Coordinator review workflow (approve / return for rework)\n\n## Spawning Tracked Background Runs\n\n**Tracked background children are separate CLI processes** (`claude -p`, `codex exec`, or `qwen` stream-JSON), each with its own context window. They are not internal sub-agents. Use explicit `task-board spawn` model selection to launch with automatic board integration and `RUN-...` tracking.\n\n**`--role`, `--background`, and `--model` are required; Codex and Qwen also require `--reasoning-effort`.** The role drives identity, skills, status lifecycle, and definition of done. See [`.specs/spawn-model-selection.md`](.specs/spawn-model-selection.md) for the no-default invariant.\n\nAgent type is a separate precedence layer. Canonical `preferred_agentic_system` enforces an exclusive pin or mixed allow-set; explicit `--agent` wins only inside it, and mixed otherwise uses in-set runtime affinity. An active UTC schedule window replaces that base system. Without the canonical system or active window, a usable legacy `spawn.preferred_agent` is selected and otherwise runtime affinity applies (`codex` from Codex, `claude` from Claude). A legacy preferred provider without a configured ceiling or executable falls back to affinity and records the path. After provider choice, the matching per-agent/role ceiling resolves model and any Codex/Qwen effort; a ceiling never changes the provider.\n\nUse `task-board q 'project_config()'` as the preflight check before spawn or spawn-contract review. `spawn.enabled=false` is an absolute project gate; `model_criterion=equal` forces the configured per-agent model without ranking it and forbids selection assessments. `spawn.ceilings.<agent>.roles.<role>` may override `model`, `model_criterion`, and `reasoning_effort` field by field for that role, with omitted fields inherited from the agent ceiling. `less_or_equal` and `greater_or_equal` compare provider-local `policyRank` values, require a deliberate one-line `--selection-rationale`, record structural `(pair, text)` history per task and role, and require exact content-bound `--ack-resolved` plus whitespace-normalized fresh rationale text after a requested-to-resolved change. Treat [`references/tracked-background-spawn.md`](references/tracked-background-spawn.md) as the canonical operator-facing contract. The durable wait/completion invariant lives in [`.specs/spawn-runtime-completion.md`](.specs/spawn-runtime-completion.md).\n\n`project_config().codex.session.auto_continue` surfaces the effective Codex anti-stall defaults for this cwd. Those settings currently apply only to the optional `task-board codex` session enhancement; they do not restrict goal-bound Codex spawn from an `agents-infra codex` primary session. Explicit `task-board codex --auto-continue*` flags override config when provided.\n\n**Template-based prompt composition is board-centric:** `spawn` composes the prompt from the role template plus board state:\n- task description comes from the element README\n- task-specific DoD comes from checklist items already present on the element\n- role baseline DoD is appended after task-specific DoD\n- precondition resources carry additional instructions and artifacts\n\nHidden legacy `--task-path` / `--dod-path` flags are compatibility-only appenders. They are not the normal workflow and should not be treated as required task inputs.\n\nIf the element has no task-specific DoD on the board, spawn should fail with a clear validation error instead of launching an underspecified agent.\n\nThe prompt also includes:\n1. Role config (identity, skills, DoD, status commands)\n2. Task README fields (title, scope, AC)\n3. Precondition resources attached to the task (loaded and injected into the prompt)\n4. A Go template (project-local `.agents/spawn-template.md` or embedded default)\n\n**Orchestrator monitoring:** A valid explicit spawn creates a tracked background runner, persists run state under `.temp/spawn-runs/`, and returns a `RUN-...` identifier immediately. The response includes coordinator-facing observation hints (text and `--json observation.*`) with a seeded resume cursor (`RUN-ID:0`) and watch/events/wait commands that preserve the active executable plus board context. `spawn events` replays durable events, `spawn watch` follows the stream, and `spawn wait` observes persisted terminal state. Watcher notifications do not inject text into the active orchestrator session unless a runtime-specific adapter exists. Codex sessions launched through optional `task-board codex` get terminal updates pushed into the active thread; this session UX does not govern goal-bound owner launch. Claude Code sessions get the same non-blocking effect by running `spawn wait RUN-...` as a harness background task that wakes the session on child completion. The background runner blocks on the real child, executes the completion hook, and only then marks the run terminal.\n\n**Project spawn policy:** local profiles use `task-board.config.json -> spawn`; remote profiles use `task-board.config.json -> remote.spawn` and fall back to root `spawn` when `remote.spawn` is missing or `null` for backward compatibility.\n`spawn.enabled` defaults to `true`; `false` rejects every spawn before board\nlookup, mutation, prompt/manifest creation, or runner registration.\n`spawn.launch_composition.enabled` defaults to `true`; explicit `false` is the\nonly opt-out. After provider selection, task-board invokes\n`agents-infra compose --agent codex|claude|qwen --project DIR --schema-version 1 --json`\nonce for a new spawn, validates the complete MCP-only contract and provider\nallowlist, and prepends the accepted prefix to ordinary and goal-bound child\nlaunches. Missing, pre-contract, unsupported, failing, or invalid infra is an\noptional-capability degradation: launch the unchanged bare child and persist a\nsanitized `disabled`, `empty`, `applied`, or `degraded_*` status. Do not retry\ncomposition in v1 and do not fail the spawn for this capability.\n\n**Project MCP inheritance is a soft `relux-agents-infra` dependency.** It\nrequires `agents-infra compose` to implement\n`agents-infra.child-launch-composition` schema version `1` and return a\nsuccessful (`status: ok`) JSON document. `scripts/setup.sh` probes that exact\nCodex-shaped command and warns—not fails—when the binary is absent or the\nresponse is incompatible. Board installation and bare child launch remain\nfully functional in that state; only MCP inheritance is skipped, with\nsanitized launch-composition evidence recorded for the child.\n\nThe private run manifest may carry the validated MCP argv snapshot for the\ndetached runner and its restart/reroute/recovery successors. Public JSON under\n`launchComposition`, task notes, state, and events must omit argv, URLs,\ncommands, command arguments, headers, stderr, and environment values. Preserve\nonly MCP server names and referenced environment-variable names; bearer values\nremain solely in the inherited child process environment. Never apply\nprimary-session model, reasoning, profile, approval, sandbox, service-tier,\nprompt, or goal settings from infra composition.\n`spawn.max_parallel` limits how many tracked background runners may actively\nexecute child-agent work at once in one local work directory; when omitted, the default limit is `20`. It is not a cross-client remote-board scheduler.\nCanonical `spawn.preferred_agentic_system` selects and constrains the provider\nbefore ceiling resolution: exclusive pins one; mixed accepts an explicit in-set\nprovider or in-set affinity. Without it, legacy usable `spawn.preferred_agent`\nand then runtime affinity apply. A provider ceiling gates only legacy preference\nusability; after selection, ceilings constrain rather than replace the provider.\nFor each provider, `spawn.ceilings.<agent>.model_criterion: equal` forces the\nconfigured registered model and records the exact-model adjustment without any\n`policyRank` comparison. `less_or_equal` clamps above the configured\nprovider-local rank and `greater_or_equal` raises below it; ordered selections\nuse the deliberate rationale and adjusted-resolution acknowledgement contract.\nOptional `spawn.ceilings.<agent>.roles.<role>` objects override the agent\nceiling field by field for that spawn role. `project_config()` reports the raw\nconfigured overrides and `spawn.config_key_prefix`, while spawn selection audits\nreport the merged effective ceiling and the role-level config key.\n\n**Codex anti-stall policy:** project-local defaults for interactive Codex sessions live in `task-board.config.json -> codex.session.auto_continue`. Use that section for team defaults, and override per launch with `task-board codex --auto-continue`, `--auto-continue-delay`, `--auto-continue-max-nudges`, or `--auto-continue-prompt`. This v1 scope is `task-board codex` only, not generic `spawn --agent codex`.\n\n**Observed load-smoke:** with `spawn.max_parallel = 20`, a local smoke run of `30` fake-codex tasks yielded `20 running / 10 queued` on the first sample, then `20 completed / 10 running / 0 queued`, then full completion with `0` failures. Treat this as the expected queue shape for small homogeneous tasks.\n\nFor spawn flags, audit hygiene, template customization, orchestrator patterns, failure modes, and manual launch fallback — see [`references/tracked-background-spawn.md`](references/tracked-background-spawn.md).\n\n---\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
